  "internal_read_at_seal": false,
  "source_independent_solution": "Source 94 asks for the graphs of y=6/x and xy=-12. By definitions 11 and 14, a plotted pair must satisfy its equation and the graph is the set of all such pairs. For y=6/x this set is {(x,6/x): x is real and x!=0}; y!=0 too. It has an entire branch for every x>0 in quadrant I and another for every x<0 in quadrant III. In particular (2,3) and (-2,-3) both have xy=6. For xy=-12, neither variable can be zero, and the full set is {(x,-12/x): x is real and x!=0}, in quadrants IV for x>0 and II for x<0; (3,-4) and (-3,4) both have xy=-12. Definition 15 identifies both nonzero-product curves as hyperbolas. As |x| tends to infinity, y tends to 0 but never reaches it; as x tends to 0 from either side, |y| tends to infinity. Thus y=0 and x=0 are asymptotes, and neither axis has a point on either graph. Negative-x branches cannot be discarded. Sign determines quadrants, but the products 6 and -12 additionally determine coordinate scale.",
  "question_review": {
    "question_id": "q-hyperbola-pair",
    "task_fidelity": {
      "verdict": "pass",
      "evidence": "The paired complete-graph descriptions require hyperbola type, both signed branches, exclusion of both axes, both axis asymptotes and numerical pairs satisfying the two original equations. A and D have identical quadrant/asymptote statements and the same x coordinates, but their y coordinates differ by a factor of ten. Knowing only signs or reciprocal-curve category cannot distinguish A from D; a coordinate/product scale check is necessary. Replacing the constants 6,-12 by 60,-120 makes D, not A, true with the existing options. This is recognition of graph properties and scale, not evidence that the learner independently constructed, selected axes/scales for or drew the full curves. That response-format loss must be declared after internal comparison; reasonable elimination is not itself a fidelity defect."
    },
